<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What the macOS bundle asks of the system, and nothing else.

  Every key here is a hardened-runtime or sandbox switch that any signature may
  carry. That constraint is the whole point: this project signs ad-hoc
  (`signingIdentity: "-"`, see docs/RELEASE.md), an ad-hoc signature has no
  Team ID, and a *restricted* entitlement — one that needs a provisioning
  profile to be legitimate — makes macOS refuse to launch the app at all.

  It did refuse. 17.0.0 shipped a `keychain-access-groups` entry with the
  literal value `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)com.hxddh.pagewise`, because an ad-hoc
  signature has no App ID prefix to substitute. 17.0.1 began signing, which was
  the first time that entry actually reached a signature — and the app stopped
  opening on Apple Silicon with "the application can't be opened", a different
  message from the unsigned build's "is damaged", for a different reason:
  AMFI kills the process at exec ("its use of the keychain-access-groups
  entitlement is not allowed", error -67050), so nothing ever runs to write a
  crash log. `scripts/check-entitlements.mjs` now fails the build if a
  restricted key is added back.

  The removed entry was never needed. `keyring`'s macOS backend uses the
  file-based login keychain (`SecKeychainAddGenericPassword` through
  `security-framework`), which is per-user and needs no keychain access group;
  access groups exist for *sharing* items between apps of one team.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- Not sandboxed: the app reads documents the reader picks, and the sandbox
       would need a bookmarks dance to keep them after a restart. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
  <false/>
  <!-- The webview's JavaScript engine. Under the hardened runtime a missing
       allow-jit caps the reader's UI at the interpreter. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <!-- Ad-hoc signatures have no Team ID, and the hardened runtime's library
       validation matches loaded libraries against that Team ID. Any non-Apple
       library we ship would be rejected as "different Team IDs"; this is the
       entitlement that tells the loader not to make that comparison. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
